.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt DISK 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm disk ,
.Nm bootpart ,
.Nm grubcore
.Nd raw disk, partition, and boot devices
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/diskN
.Pp
.Pa /dev/diskNpM
.Pp
.Pa /dev/bootpart
.Pp
.Pa /dev/grubcore
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa /dev/diskN
accesses disk N, and
.Pa /dev/diskNpM
accesses partition M of that disk.
Disk numbers follow registration order; released numbers are reused
from the lowest available number.
Partition numbers are 1 through 4 for MBR primary entries, 5 upward
for logical partitions in chain order, and the array position counted
from 1 for GPT entries.
The MBR extended entry has no node.
.Pp
Every boot publishes a whole-disk node for each disk it registered, and a
USB stick's node is published when the stick is found.
The recovery kernel, and any kernel booted with
.Ql rach2.diskdiag
on its command line, also probe for a disk the boot did not register.
A whole-disk window covers the disk's full reported sector count.
The nodes are block devices with mode 0400, accessible only to root.
Reads accept any byte range.
A read from a pulled stick fails with
.Er EIO .
.Pp
Only the recovery kernel accepts writes through whole-disk and partition
nodes, and each write requires effective user ID 0.
The ordinary kernel returns
.Er EPERM .
Write offsets and lengths must be multiples of the 512-byte sector size.
A raw write to a write-protected stick fails with
.Er EIO .
.Pp
ATA and AHCI flush the device cache after each successful write command,
and NVMe sends each raw write with force unit access, so a successful raw
write to any of them is durable on return.
USB raw writes issue ordinary SCSI writes without a device-cache flush;
success does not guarantee that the data is durable.
.Ss Boot devices
.Pa /dev/bootpart
accesses entry 1 of the selected boot disk's table, MBR or GUID.
The entry must describe a FAT32 partition starting at or after LBA 2048,
smaller than 2 GiB, and overlapping no other partition.
.Pa /dev/grubcore
accesses, on an MBR disk, LBA 1 through 2047 when no partition overlaps
that range, and on a GUID disk the sectors of its one BIOS boot partition,
clear of sector zero, the headers and the entry array; offset zero is the
partition's first sector, LBA 34 on the disks this system builds.
Neither includes sector zero.
.Pp
Both boot devices are character devices with mode 0600.
Their reads and writes require sector-aligned offsets and lengths.
They accept root's writes on ordinary and recovery kernels.
The metal kernel's read-only ATA, AHCI and NVMe driver policy prevents writes
through them as well as other writes to those disks.
.Ss Reservations and mounts
Each disk permits one write-capable open file description across its
whole-disk node and the two boot devices in every build, and across its
partition nodes as well on the recovery kernel.
The boot devices share the selected boot disk's reservation.
A second write-capable open on that disk fails with
.Er EBUSY .
.Pa /dev/grubcore
also permits only one open file description at a time, including readers.
.Pp
A mount claims its sector range.
A write-capable open overlapping that range fails with
.Er EBUSY ,
on the recovery kernel for partition nodes too;
a mount overlapping an open writer fails with the same error.
The claims also exclude overlapping whole-disk and partition mounts.
.Ss Partition table reread
The
.Dv BLKRRPART
request on a whole-disk node rereads the partition table and rebuilds
the partition nodes.
It fails with
.Er EBUSY
while the disk or a partition is mounted or another independently
opened whole-disk or partition-node description exists on that disk,
in the calling process included; duplicated descriptors and boot-device
opens are not counted.
On a partition node it fails with
.Er ENOTTY .
.Sh FILES
All these devices use major number 240.
.Bl -tag -width /dev/diskNpM
.It Pa /dev/diskN
Minor 1 for disk 0; minor 64 + 16N for later disks.
.It Pa /dev/diskNpM
Minors 4 through 7 for disk 0's first four partitions;
minor 64 + M for its later partitions.
For later disks, the minor is 64 + 16N + M.
.It Pa /dev/bootpart
Minor 0.
.It Pa /dev/grubcore
Minor 3.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr loop 4 ,
.Xr nvme 4 ,
.Xr disk 7 ,
.Xr bootctl 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr reflash 8
